{"id":"qrs-slope-energy","t":"Slope energy for QRS detection","tags":["Time Series","Feature Engineering"],"d":"Easy","rt":"numpy","fn":"slope_energy","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"QRS complexes are the steepest part of an ECG. Turn that into a detection signal, in the spirit of the Pan-Tompkins method.\n\nWrite `slope_energy(x, fs, win_s)`:\n1. Difference: $d_n = x_n - x_{n-1}$, with $d_0 = 0$.\n2. Square: $s_n = d_n^2$.\n3. Trailing moving average over $w$ = `int(win_s * fs)` samples: $e_n$ is the mean of $s_{n-w+1}, \\dots, s_n$, using only the available samples near the start (so $e_0 = s_0$, $e_1 = (s_0+s_1)/2$, …).\n\nReturn $e$ as a list.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef slope_energy(x, fs, win_s):\n    \"\"\"Squared first difference, smoothed by a trailing moving average.\"\"\"\n    d = np.diff(np.asarray(x, float))\n    return (d ** 2).tolist()\n","hints":["A cumulative sum turns every window mean into one subtraction.","Squaring makes the signal positive and emphasises large slopes over small ones.","The window should be about the width of a QRS complex (around 0.15 s); too short and one beat gives several peaks, too long and it merges with the T wave."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef slope_energy(x, fs, win_s):\n    x = np.asarray(x, float)\n    d = np.r_[0.0, np.diff(x)]\n    s = d ** 2\n    w = max(int(win_s * fs), 1)\n    c = np.r_[0.0, np.cumsum(s)]\n    n = np.arange(1, len(s) + 1)\n    lo = np.maximum(n - w, 0)\n    return ((c[n] - c[lo]) / (n - lo)).tolist()\n","ex_md":"## Why slope, not height\nTall T waves, artefacts and baseline drift can all reach the height of an R wave. Very few things are as steep. Differencing picks out the fast up- and down-strokes of the QRS, squaring makes them dominant, and the moving average merges the several slope peaks of one complex into a single hump per beat.\n\n## The delay\nThe trailing window shifts each hump later than the R peak by roughly half the window. A detector that finds humps in this signal must look back in the filtered ECG to place the R peak exactly, which the capstone does.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Pan and Tompkins band-pass filter the ECG (roughly 5 to 15 Hz) before differencing. What does that remove that differencing alone would amplify?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"[0.0, 1.0, 1.0, 3.0]\n1\n2","out":"[0.0, 0.5, 0.5, 2.0]"},{"in":"[0.0, 1.0, 1.0, 3.0]\n1\n1","out":"[0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 4.0]"}],"lessons":[],"related":["baseline-wander-removal"],"ntests":5,"tj":"[{\"a\": [[0.0, 1.0, 1.0, 3.0], 1, 2], \"e\": [0.0, 0.5, 0.5, 2.0]}, {\"a\": [[0.0, 1.0, 1.0, 3.0], 1, 1], \"e\": [0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 4.0]}, {\"a\": [[5.0], 10, 0.5], \"e\": [0.0]}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [0.006911683841295721, 0.016432362870023167, 0.006608741523667742, -0.02606314463208722, 0.018107117333462354, 0.008927491447280226, -0.010739064707205703, 0.011622362083927062, 0.007291447923721515, 0.00588264993311052, 0.0005684448263159358, 0.010934259732248939, -0.014729081740033338, -0.0032581989598610556, -0.009642386253599566, 0.01197692425269255, 0.0007944421496331798, -0.005849135019301772, -0.015638169247136843, -0.005143844812377414, 0.00016284361036687016, -0.005512058105987408, 0.025881276287964147, 0.020134486306115886, -0.05422324957931937, -0.037780264919353455, -0.003495441841103239, -0.00844380823152707, 0.004272859949972222, 0.004346438620451272, 0.04235677510102097, -0.022240415253845628, -0.0075521001425399616, 0.04085543214984827, 0.012934059937924888, 0.013261296298815875, -0.010265182095223738, -0.031031049280878163, 0.06552581890663586, 0.5015320703562482, 0.975452958919275, 0.4856872553932467, 0.06073565062141539, -0.016964577152306284, -0.0019504474696461786, 0.001909723161232075, 0.000711885415431742, -0.010125127393724627, 0.01187782348457838, 0.017834052940539056, 0.006453989032557278, -0.01624650383560962, 0.014980814427880193, -0.009083466889807714, 0.019955328581237743, -0.01594105667092862, 0.030038512592271777, 0.02279015304068021, 0.017282548469625042, 0.0648